===== docs/plans/2026-10-06-csat-rating.md =====
# Customer Satisfaction Rating Implementation Plan

> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.

**Goal:** Let a customer rate a resolved ticket 1-5 from the portal, notify the owning agent on a low score, publish a nightly per-agent weekly summary, and rename `assignee_id` to `owner_id` throughout the codebase.

**Architecture:** Add a `ratings` Map to the in-memory `store.js` (same shape as the existing `messages` Map, keyed by ticket id). Add a `rateTicket` portal action next to `reply`/`getMyTicket`, following the existing validate-then-mutate pattern used by `resolveTicket`/`reopenTicket`. Add a fourth nightly job, `summarize-ratings.js`, following the existing job pattern (`close-resolved.js`, `purge-closed.js`): a plain function taking `(store, { now })`, looping and filtering by age in milliseconds. The `assignee_id` → `owner_id` rename is a small, mechanical, expand-only change done last so the rating code is written once, against the final field name.

**Tech Stack:** Node.js 20, `node:test` + `node:assert/strict` (no dependencies), the existing in-memory `store.js`.

**Source verified directly for this plan:** `src/store.js`, `src/tickets.js`, `src/portal.js`, `src/notify.js`, `src/jobs/export-tickets.js`, `src/jobs/close-resolved.js`, `src/jobs/purge-closed.js`, `docs/glossary.md`, `README.md`, `test/tickets.test.js`, `test/export-tickets.test.js`, `package.json`.

---

## Decisions and recommendations — needs your sign-off

The spec leaves several implementation choices open. Each one below has a recommendation already applied in the tasks that follow. Skim this list when you're back; if you disagree with any of them, say so and the affected task(s) get adjusted before anything is built — nothing below has been built yet.

**D1. Check order inside `rateTicket`: wrong org/missing → wrong status → bad score → duplicate → write.**
Why this order: it mirrors the existing precedent — `getMyTicket` already returns `null` for an invisible ticket (used as-is by `reply`), and `resolveTicket`/`reopenTicket` check ticket status before anything else. Bad-score and duplicate aren't ordered by any existing precedent; recommendation is score-shape (cheapest, input-level check) before the duplicate-rating check (a store lookup). No acceptance test exercises two violations at once, so this ordering is only observable if you call `rateTicket` with *multiple* things wrong — low risk either way.

**D2. The exactly-7-day-old rating is excluded from the weekly summary.**
The spec pins the 8-day-old case ("not counted") but not the 7-day boundary itself. Recommendation: exclude it — i.e. the job keeps a rating only when `now - rated_at < 7 days`, same direction as `close-resolved.js`'s own `< QUIET_DAYS * DAY` check. A test pins this exact boundary (Task 4) so the behavior is locked in regardless of which way you'd prefer, and is a one-line flip if you want the boundary the other way.

**D3. A resolved-but-unassigned ticket rated 1 or 2 skips the notification instead of throwing.**
Mirrors the existing guard in `reopenTicket` (`src/tickets.js:42`, `if (t.assignee_id && ...)`) for the same situation — a resolved ticket can be unassigned, since nothing in `resolveTicket` requires an assignee. Recommendation: same guard, same silent skip, no new error path.

**D4. `exports/tickets.json` (read by finance, outside this repo) gets both `owner_id` and a legacy `assignee_id` key (same value) — dual-write, not a clean rename.**
The spec says rename "everywhere," but `src/jobs/export-tickets.js:3`'s comment says this file is read by finance's own reporting job, deployed on their schedule, which we don't control. A clean rename risks silently breaking their job the night this ships. Recommendation: keep both keys in the exported JSON only (the in-memory store and all in-repo code use `owner_id` exclusively); drop `assignee_id` from the export in a later, separate change once finance confirms they've moved to `owner_id`. If finance doesn't exist as a real external consumer (i.e. this comment is stale), skip Task 6's dual-write and do a clean rename there too — tell me and I'll fold that in.

**D5. `rateTicket` lives in `src/portal.js`, next to `reply`.**
It's a customer-facing action that needs the same org-ownership check as `reply`/`getMyTicket` (`t.org_id !== customer.orgId`), not an agent-facing action like the functions in `tickets.js`. Recommendation: `portal.js`, no new file.

**D6. New job file is `src/jobs/summarize-ratings.js`; its tests (and `rateTicket`'s) live in a new `test/ratings.test.js`, not added to `test/tickets.test.js`.**
Naming matches the existing verb-first job convention (`close-resolved`, `purge-closed`, `export-tickets`). A separate test file is recommended because this feature is large enough (rating + notification + summary job) to warrant its own fixtures, versus `close-resolved`/`purge-closed`, which are small enough to share `tickets.test.js`'s existing ticket-lifecycle fixtures.

**D7. The exported weekly summary rounds `average_score` to 2 decimal places** (`Math.round(x * 100) / 100`), matching the spec's own example (`5, 4, 1` → `3.33`, not `3.3333333333333335`).

**D8. The glossary's "Assignee" entry is renamed to "Owner" (heading and body wording), not just the code field.**
The spec only asks to rename the *field*, but `docs/glossary.md` exists specifically so the words used in code, the back office, and to customers all agree — leaving the heading as "Assignee" once the field is `owner_id` would immediately reintroduce the mismatch the glossary is meant to prevent. A one-line "Rating" entry is added alongside it for the same reason (it's a new concept the glossary doesn't yet define). Recommendation: do both as part of Task 5.

**D9. Phases run rate-a-ticket → weekly job → rename, in that order** (matching the spec's own ordering, where the rename is item 6, "while we are here"). Each phase leaves `node --test` green on its own, so the rename lands as a single, easy-to-review, mechanical diff on top of a feature that's already fully working under the old field name.

